0, plus, 2 times 24\"></span>, which is equal to 148.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the area of one of the faces of the prism. Choice B is incorrect and may result from adding the areas of three adjacent rectangles without multiplying by 2. Choice C is incorrect. This is the volume, in cubic inches, of the prism.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959627,"updateDate":1691007959627,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959627, 1691007959627),
    ($SATQ$f811d345$SATQ$, $SATQ$ae6da77a-4de7-4375-a532-b00350663a9f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7ba59adb-5da7-4566-8128-5a589f398d0e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Right triangles and trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A right triangle has legs with lengths of <math alttext="24"><mn>24</mn>
</math> centimeters and <math alttext="21"><mn>21</mn>
</math> centimeters. If the length of this triangle's hypotenuse, in centimeters, can be written in the form <math alttext="3 StartRoot d EndRoot"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></math>, where <math alttext="d"><mi>d</mi>
</math> is an integer, what is the value of <math alttext="d"><mi>d</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["113"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["113"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="113"><mn>113</mn>
</math>. It's given that the legs of a right triangle have lengths <math alttext="24"><mn>24</mn>
</math> centimeters and <math alttext="21"><mn>21</mn>
</math> centimeters. In a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the two legs. It follows that if <math alttext="h"><mi>h</mi>
</math> represents the length, in centimeters, of the hypotenuse of the right triangle, <math alttext="h squared equals 24 squared plus 21 squared"><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>24</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>21</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. This equation is equivalent to <math alttext="h squared equals 1,017"><mrow>
	<msup>
		<mi>h</mi>
		<mn>2</mn>
	</msup>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>1,017</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Taking the square root of each side of this equation yields <math alttext="h equals StartRoot 1,017 EndRoot"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>1,017</mn></msqrt></math>. This equation can be rewritten as <math alttext="h equals StartRoot 9 dot 113 EndRoot"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>9</mn><mo>·</mo><mn>113</mn></msqrt></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="h equals StartRoot 9 EndRoot dot StartRoot 113 EndRoot"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>9</mn></msqrt><mo>·</mo><msqrt><mn>113</mn></msqrt></math>. This equation is equivalent to <math alttext="h equals 3 StartRoot 113 EndRoot"><mrow>
	<mi>h</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mn>3</mn>
		<msqrt>
			<mn>113</mn>
		</msqrt>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. It's given that the length of the triangle's hypotenuse, in centimeters, can be written in the form <math alttext="3 StartRoot d EndRoot"><mrow>
	<mn>3</mn>
	<msqrt>
		<mi>d</mi>
	</msqrt>
</mrow>
</math>. It follows that the value of <math alttext="d"><mi>d</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="113"><mn>113</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f811d345","external_id":"ae6da77a-4de7-4375-a532-b00350663a9f","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"7ba59adb-5da7-4566-8128-5a589f398d0e","uId":"8c39fe52-5b23-4791-8465-eb2d6a7a9293","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.C.","skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A right triangle has legs with lengths of <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> centimeters and <math alttext=\"21\"><mn>21</mn>\n</math> centimeters. If the length of this triangle's hypotenuse, in centimeters, can be written in the form <math alttext=\"3 StartRoot d EndRoot\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></math>, where <math alttext=\"d\"><mi>d</mi>\n</math> is an integer, what is the value of <math alttext=\"d\"><mi>d</mi>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["113"],"correct_answer":["113"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"113\"><mn>113</mn>\n</math>. It's given that the legs of a right triangle have lengths <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> centimeters and <math alttext=\"21\"><mn>21</mn>\n</math> centimeters. In a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the two legs. It follows that if <math alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi>\n</math> represents the length, in centimeters, of the hypotenuse of the right triangle, <math alttext=\"h squared equals 24 squared plus 21 squared\"><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>24</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>21</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. This equation is equivalent to <math alttext=\"h squared equals 1,017\"><mrow>\n\t<msup>\n\t\t<mi>h</mi>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</