    ($SATQ$fd8745fc$SATQ$, $SATQ$7ad5d0d9-6334-465a-9284-026de00b1ddf$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$286294bf-678f-48d8-b9d4-acb344623cb0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Lines, angles, and triangles$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">In triangle&nbsp;<math alttext="upper J upper K upper L"><mi>J</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></math>, the measures of&nbsp;<math alttext="angle upper K"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>K</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext="angle upper L"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>L</mi></math> are each <math alttext="48 degree"><mrow><mn>48</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure of <math alttext="angle upper J"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>J</mi></math>, in degrees? (Disregard the degree symbol when entering your answer.)</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["84"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["84"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="84"><mn>84</mn>
</math>. The sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is <math alttext="180 degree"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's given that in triangle <math alttext="upper J upper K upper L"><mi>J</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></math>, the measures of <math alttext="angle upper K"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>K</mi></math>and&nbsp;<math alttext="angle upper L"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>L</mi></math> are each <math alttext="48 degree"><mn>48</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Adding the measures, in degrees, of <math alttext="angle upper K"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>K</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext="angle upper L"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>L</mi></math> gives <math alttext="48 plus 48"><mn>48</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>48</mn></math>, or <math alttext="96"><mn>96</mn>
</math>. Therefore, the measure of&nbsp;<math alttext="angle upper J"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>J</mi></math>, in degrees, is <math alttext="180 minus 96"><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>96</mn></math>, or <math alttext="84"><mn>84</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"fd8745fc","external_id":"7ad5d0d9-6334-465a-9284-026de00b1ddf","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"286294bf-678f-48d8-b9d4-acb344623cb0","uId":"5d3c59ea-291e-45b1-94ae-39dd38130482","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.B.","skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle&nbsp;<math alttext=\"upper J upper K upper L\"><mi>J</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></math>, the measures of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper K\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>K</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper L\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>L</mi></math> are each <math alttext=\"48 degree\"><mrow><mn>48</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper J\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>J</mi></math>, in degrees? (Disregard the degree symbol when entering your answer.)</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["84"],"correct_answer":["84"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"84\"><mn>84</mn>\n</math>. The sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is <math alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's given that in triangle <math alttext=\"upper J upper K upper L\"><mi>J</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></math>, the measures of <math alttext=\"angle upper K\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>K</mi></math>and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper L\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>L</mi></math> are each <math alttext=\"48 degree\"><mn>48</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Adding the measures, in degrees, of <math alttext=\"angle upper K\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>K</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper L\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>L</mi></math> gives <math alttext=\"48 plus 48\"><mn>48</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>48</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"96\"><mn>96</mn>\n</math>. Therefore, the measure of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper J\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>J</mi></math>, in degrees, is <math alttext=\"180 minus 96\"><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>96</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"84\"><mn>84</mn>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959838,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"fd8745fc","skill_cd":"S.B.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","createDate":1691007959838,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":null,"external_id":"7ad5d0d9-6334-465a-9284-026de00b1ddf","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"5d3c59ea-291e-45b1-94ae-39dd38130482","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["84"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"84\"><mn>84</mn>\n</math>. The sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is <math alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's given that in triangle <math alttext=\"upper J upper K upper L\"><mi>J</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></math>, the measures of <math alttext=\"angle upper K\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>K</mi></math>and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper L\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>L</mi></math> are each <math alttext=\"48 degree\"><mn>48</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Adding the measures, in degrees, of <math alttext=\"angle upper K\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>K</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper L\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>L</mi></math> gives <math alttext=\"48 plus 48\"><mn>48</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>48</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"96\"><mn>96</mn>\n</math>. Therefore, the measure of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper J\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>J</mi></math>, in degrees, is <math alttext=\"180 minus 96\"><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>96</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"84\"><mn>84</mn>\n</math>.</p>","origin":"manifold","s