    ($SATQ$fecc446d$SATQ$, $SATQ$a8b595d8-c45d-47e6-9ef2-b8a5d7a44e1c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Lines, angles, and triangles$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A line intersects two parallel lines, forming four acute angles and four obtuse angles. The measure of one of these eight angles is <math alttext="left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree"><mfenced><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. The sum of the measures of four of the eight angles is <math alttext="k degree"><mi>k</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Which of the following could NOT be equivalent to <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math>, for all values of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"dd054543-4140-4650-9c41-f9a136affce7","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus 14 x plus 1,540\"><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,540</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"d860abb3-0ed7-4b87-8b2c-68b65c364784","content":"<p><math alttext=\"14 x minus 320\"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>320</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"9f1ef835-2252-4b0b-9e34-8cf7c78f6ff9","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus 28 x plus 1,720\"><mo>-</mo><mn>28</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,720</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"2c4bd8b3-fc32-465d-87a1-859ab66a3d20","content":"<p><math alttext=\"360\"><mn>360</mn></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dd054543-4140-4650-9c41-f9a136affce7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is correct. It&rsquo;s given that a line intersects two parallel lines, forming four acute angles and four obtuse angles. Since there are two parallel lines intersected by a transversal, all four acute angles have the same measure and all four obtuse angles have the same measure. Additionally, each acute angle is supplementary to each obtuse angle. It&rsquo;s given that the measure of one of these eight angles is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that a supplementary angle has measure <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 180 minus left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis right parenthesis degree"><mo>(</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It&rsquo;s also given that the sum of the measures of four of the eight angles is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="k degree"><mi>k</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that the possible values of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="k"><mi>k</mi></math> are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="4 left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis"><mn>4</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis plus 3 left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="2 left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis plus 2 left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis"><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3 left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis plus left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis"><mn>3</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="4 left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis"><mn>4</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. These values are equivalent to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="28 x minus 1,000"><mn>28</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1,000</mn></math>; <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="minus 14 x plus 1,040"><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,040</mn></math>; <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="360"><mn>360</mn></math>; <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="14 x minus 320"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>320</mn></math>; and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="minus 28 x plus 1,720"><mo>-</mo><mn>28</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,720</mn></math>, respectively. It follows that of the given choices, only <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="minus 14 x plus 1,540"><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,540</mn></math> could NOT be equivalent to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="k"><mi>k</mi></math>, for all values of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. This is the sum of three angles with measure <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;and one angle with measure <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the sum of four angles with measure <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. This is the sum of two angles with measure <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;and two angles with measure <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"fecc446d","external_id":"a8b595d8-c45d-47e6-9ef2-b8a5d7a44e1c","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"6899c9d2-23d1-48bd-b1bc-83c1618e4caa","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.B.","skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A line intersects two parallel lines, forming four acute angles and four obtuse angles. The measure of one of these eight angles is <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. The sum of the measures of four of the eight angles is <math alttext=\"k degree\"><mi>k</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Which of the following could NOT be equivalent to <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>, for all values of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"dd054543-4140-4650-9c41-f9a136affce7","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus 14 x plus 1,540\"><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,540</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"d860abb3-0ed7-4b87-8b2c-68b65c364784","content":"<p><math alttext=\"14 x minus 320\"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>320</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"9f1ef835-2252-4b0b-9e34-8cf7c78f6ff9","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus 28 x plus 1,720\"><mo>-</mo><mn>28</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,720</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"2c4bd8b3-fc32-465d-87a1-859ab66a3d20","content":"<p><math alttext=\"360\"><mn>360</mn></math></p>"}],"keys":["dd054543-4140-4650-9c41-f9a136affce7"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is correct. It&rsquo;s given that a line intersects two parallel lines, forming four acute angles and four obtuse angles. Since there are two parallel lines intersected by a transversal, all four acute angles have the same measure and all four obtuse angles have the same measure. Additionally, each acute angle is supplementary to each obtuse angle. It&rsquo;s given that the measure of one of these eight angles is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that a supplementary angle has measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 180 minus left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It&rsquo;s also given that the sum of the measures of four of the eight angles is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"k degree\"><mi>k</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that the possible values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4 left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis\"><mn>4</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis plus 3 left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis plus 2 left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis\"><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3 left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis plus left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis\"><mn>3</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4 left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis\"><mn>4</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. These values are equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"28 x minus 1,000\"><mn>28</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1,000</mn></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"minus 14 x plus 1,040\"><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,040</mn></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"360\"><mn>360</mn></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14 x minus 320\"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>320</mn></math>; and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"minus 28 x plus 1,720\"><mo>-</mo><mn>28</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,720</mn></math>, respectively. It follows that of the given choices, only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"minus 14 x plus 1,540\"><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,540</mn></math> could NOT be equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi></math>, for all values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the sum of three angles with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;and one angle with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the sum of four angles with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the sum of two angles with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;and two angles with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017722,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"fecc446d","skill_cd":"S.B.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","createDate":1755117017722,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"a8b595d8-c45d-47e6-9ef2-b8a5d7a44e1c","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"6899c9d2-23d1-48bd-b1bc-83c1618e4caa","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"mcq","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A line intersects two parallel lines, forming four acute angles and four obtuse angles. The measure of one of these eight angles is <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. The sum of the measures of four of the eight angles is <math alttext=\"k degree\"><mi>k</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Which of the following could NOT be equivalent to <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>, for all values of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["dd054543-4140-4650-9c41-f9a136affce7"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"dd054543-4140-4650-9c41-f9a136affce7","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus 14 x plus 1,540\"><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,540</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"d860abb3-0ed7-4b87-8b2c-68b65c364784","content":"<p><math alttext=\"14 x minus 320\"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>320</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"9f1ef835-2252-4b0b-9e34-8cf7c78f6ff9","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus 28 x plus 1,720\"><mo>-</mo><mn>28</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,720</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"2c4bd8b3-fc32-465d-87a1-859ab66a3d20","content":"<p><math alttext=\"360\"><mn>360</mn></math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is correct. It&rsquo;s given that a line intersects two parallel lines, forming four acute angles and four obtuse angles. Since there are two parallel lines intersected by a transversal, all four acute angles have the same measure and all four obtuse angles have the same measure. Additionally, each acute angle is supplementary to each obtuse angle. It&rsquo;s given that the measure of one of these eight angles is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that a supplementary angle has measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 180 minus left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It&rsquo;s also given that the sum of the measures of four of the eight angles is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"k degree\"><mi>k</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that the possible values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4 left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis\"><mn>4</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis plus 3 left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis plus 2 left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis\"><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3 left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis plus left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis\"><mn>3</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>; and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4 left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis\"><mn>4</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. These values are equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"28 x minus 1,000\"><mn>28</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1,000</mn></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"minus 14 x plus 1,040\"><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,040</mn></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"360\"><mn>360</mn></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14 x minus 320\"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>320</mn></math>; and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"minus 28 x plus 1,720\"><mo>-</mo><mn>28</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,720</mn></math>, respectively. It follows that of the given choices, only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"minus 14 x plus 1,540\"><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1,540</mn></math> could NOT be equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi></math>, for all values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the sum of three angles with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;and one angle with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the sum of four angles with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the sum of two angles with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 x minus 250 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;and two angles with measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis minus 7 x plus 430 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>430</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>","externalid":"a8b595d8-c45d-47e6-9ef2-b8a5d7a44e1c","correct_answer":["A"]},"createDate":1755117017722,"updateDate":1755117017722}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017722, 1755117017722),
    ($SATQ$ffe862a3$SATQ$, $SATQ$4959ea4c-8faf-4a7c-9ef9-60dc6d45d751$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e5afbb81-7acf-4eec-84ed-160f331a5b69$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Right triangles and trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>An isosceles right triangle has a hypotenuse of length <math alttext="58"><mn>58</mn>
</math> inches. What is the perimeter, in inches, of this triangle?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"51488048-4717-464e-985e-0cfc8b86f4aa","content":"<p><math alttext=\"29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>29</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e56739da-819c-4913-8103-f5ac2e991b2e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"8e878c6b-4833-40f3-9233-b968ae9da370","content":"<p><math alttext=\"58 plus 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"49b6d514-24d9-4519-a2d7-38829dbc9c4f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"58 plus 116 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>116</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8e878c6b-4833-40f3-9233-b968ae9da370"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is correct. Since the triangle is an isosceles right triangle, the two sides that form the right angle must be the same length. Let <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> be the length, in inches, of each of those sides. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, <math alttext="a squared plus b squared equals c squared"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<msup>
			<mi>a</mi>
			<mn>2</mn>
		</msup>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<msup>
			<mi>b</mi>
			<mn>2</mn>
		</msup>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<msup>
		<mi>c</mi>
		<mn>2</mn>
	</msup>
</mrow>
</math>, where <math alttext="c"><mi>c</mi>
</math> is the length of the hypotenuse and <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="b"><mi>b</mi>
</math> are the lengths of the other two sides. Substituting <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> for <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math>, <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> for <math alttext="b"><mi>b</mi>
</math>, and <math alttext="58"><mn>58</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="c"><mi>c</mi>
</math> in this equation yields <math alttext="x squared plus x squared equals 58 squared"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext="2 x squared equals 58 squared"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Dividing each side of this equation by <math alttext="2"><mn>2</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="x squared equals StartFraction 58 squared Over 2 EndFraction"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>,&nbsp;or&nbsp;<math alttext="x squared equals StartFraction 2 dot 58 squared Over 4 EndFraction"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>·</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. Taking the square root of each side of this equation yields two solutions: <math alttext="x equals StartFraction 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>58</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> and <math alttext="x equals minus StartFraction 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>58</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>. The value of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> must be positive because it represents a side length. Therefore, <math alttext="x equals StartFraction 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>58</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext="x equals 29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mn>29</mn>
		<msqrt>
			<mn>2</mn>
		</msqrt>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. The perimeter, in inches, of the triangle is&nbsp;<math alttext="58 plus x plus x"><mn>58</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></math>, or <math alttext="58 plus 2 x"><mn>58</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></math>. Substituting <math alttext="29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mrow>
	<mn>29</mn>
	<msqrt>
		<mn>2</mn>
	</msqrt>
</mrow>
</math> for <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> in this expression gives a perimeter, in inches, of&nbsp;<math alttext="58 plus 2 left parenthesis 29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis"><mn>58</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>29</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext="58 plus 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mrow>
	<mn>58</mn>
	<mo>+</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mn>58</mn>
		<msqrt>
			<mn>2</mn>
		</msqrt>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. This is the length, in inches, of each of the congruent sides of the triangle, not the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. This is the sum of the lengths, in inches, of the congruent sides of the triangle, not the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ffe862a3","external_id":"4959ea4c-8faf-4a7c-9ef9-60dc6d45d751","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"e5afbb81-7acf-4eec-84ed-160f331a5b69","uId":"7150265e-6b41-431e-9bd3-c357faf60fd0","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.C.","skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>An isosceles right triangle has a hypotenuse of length <math alttext=\"58\"><mn>58</mn>\n</math> inches. What is the perimeter, in inches, of this triangle?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"51488048-4717-464e-985e-0cfc8b86f4aa","content":"<p><math alttext=\"29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>29</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e56739da-819c-4913-8103-f5ac2e991b2e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"8e878c6b-4833-40f3-9233-b968ae9da370","content":"<p><math alttext=\"58 plus 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"49b6d514-24d9-4519-a2d7-38829dbc9c4f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"58 plus 116 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>116</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["8e878c6b-4833-40f3-9233-b968ae9da370"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is correct. Since the triangle is an isosceles right triangle, the two sides that form the right angle must be the same length. Let <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> be the length, in inches, of each of those sides. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, <math alttext=\"a squared plus b squared equals c squared\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<msup>\n\t\t\t<mi>a</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msup>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<msup>\n\t\t\t<mi>b</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msup>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<msup>\n\t\t<mi>c</mi>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msup>\n</mrow>\n</math>, where <math alttext=\"c\"><mi>c</mi>\n</math> is the length of the hypotenuse and <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi>\n</math> are the lengths of the other two sides. Substituting <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi>\n</math>, and <math alttext=\"58\"><mn>58</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"c\"><mi>c</mi>\n</math> in this equation yields <math alttext=\"x squared plus x squared equals 58 squared\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext=\"2 x squared equals 58 squared\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Dividing each side of this equation by <math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"x squared equals StartFraction 58 squared Over 2 EndFraction\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>,&nbsp;or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"x squared equals StartFraction 2 dot 58 squared Over 4 EndFraction\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>·</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. Taking the square root of each side of this equation yields two solutions: <math alttext=\"x equals StartFraction 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>58</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> and <math alttext=\"x equals minus StartFraction 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>58</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>. The value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> must be positive because it represents a side length. Therefore, <math alttext=\"x equals StartFraction 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>58</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"x equals 29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>29</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. The perimeter, in inches, of the triangle is&nbsp;<math alttext=\"58 plus x plus x\"><mn>58</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></math>, or <math alttext=\"58 plus 2 x\"><mn>58</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>29</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> in this expression gives a perimeter, in inches, of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"58 plus 2 left parenthesis 29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis\"><mn>58</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>29</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"58 plus 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the length, in inches, of each of the congruent sides of the triangle, not the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the sum of the lengths, in inches, of the congruent sides of the triangle, not the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959840,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"ffe862a3","skill_cd":"S.C.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","createDate":1691007959840,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":null,"external_id":"4959ea4c-8faf-4a7c-9ef9-60dc6d45d751","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"7150265e-6b41-431e-9bd3-c357faf60fd0","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["8e878c6b-4833-40f3-9233-b968ae9da370"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is correct. Since the triangle is an isosceles right triangle, the two sides that form the right angle must be the same length. Let <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> be the length, in inches, of each of those sides. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, <math alttext=\"a squared plus b squared equals c squared\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<msup>\n\t\t\t<mi>a</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msup>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<msup>\n\t\t\t<mi>b</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msup>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<msup>\n\t\t<mi>c</mi>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msup>\n</mrow>\n</math>, where <math alttext=\"c\"><mi>c</mi>\n</math> is the length of the hypotenuse and <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi>\n</math> are the lengths of the other two sides. Substituting <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi>\n</math>, and <math alttext=\"58\"><mn>58</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"c\"><mi>c</mi>\n</math> in this equation yields <math alttext=\"x squared plus x squared equals 58 squared\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext=\"2 x squared equals 58 squared\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Dividing each side of this equation by <math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"x squared equals StartFraction 58 squared Over 2 EndFraction\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>,&nbsp;or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"x squared equals StartFraction 2 dot 58 squared Over 4 EndFraction\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>·</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. Taking the square root of each side of this equation yields two solutions: <math alttext=\"x equals StartFraction 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>58</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> and <math alttext=\"x equals minus StartFraction 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>58</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>. The value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> must be positive because it represents a side length. Therefore, <math alttext=\"x equals StartFraction 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>58</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"x equals 29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>29</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. The perimeter, in inches, of the triangle is&nbsp;<math alttext=\"58 plus x plus x\"><mn>58</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></math>, or <math alttext=\"58 plus 2 x\"><mn>58</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>29</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> in this expression gives a perimeter, in inches, of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"58 plus 2 left parenthesis 29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis\"><mn>58</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>29</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"58 plus 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the length, in inches, of each of the congruent sides of the triangle, not the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the sum of the lengths, in inches, of the congruent sides of the triangle, not the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<p>An isosceles right triangle has a hypotenuse of length <math alttext=\"58\"><mn>58</mn>\n</math> inches. What is the perimeter, in inches, of this triangle?</p>","externalid":"4959ea4c-8faf-4a7c-9ef9-60dc6d45d751","templateid":"0c77aaba-1a90-407e-bbad-a9c16bd5effc","vaultid":"e5afbb81-7acf-4eec-84ed-160f331a5b69","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"51488048-4717-464e-985e-0cfc8b86f4aa","content":"<p><math alttext=\"29 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>29</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e56739da-819c-4913-8103-f5ac2e991b2e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"8e878c6b-4833-40f3-9233-b968ae9da370","content":"<p><math alttext=\"58 plus 58 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>58</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</m